{"rows":[{"n":1,"file":"topics/01-java-core-jvm.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":2,"file":"topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":3,"file":"topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":4,"file":"topics/04-rest-grpc-webflux.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":5,"file":"topics/05-db-core-index-lock.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":6,"file":"topics/06-db-scaling.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":7,"file":"topics/07-sql-nosql-db-engines.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":8,"file":"topics/08-message-queue.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":9,"file":"topics/09-distributed-tx-fintech.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":12,"file":"topics/12-architecture-patterns.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":13,"file":"topics/13-security-oauth2.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":14,"file":"topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":15,"file":"topics/15-network-i-o-models.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":16,"file":"topics/16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.json","topic_type":"algorithm","system_design_items":["16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q1","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q2","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q3","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q4","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q5","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.whiteboard-dsa.q1"]},{"n":17,"file":"topics/17-rest-api-design.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":18,"file":"topics/18-query-optimization.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":19,"file":"topics/19-dsa-leetcode.json","topic_type":"algorithm"},{"n":20,"file":"topics/20-observability-sre.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":21,"file":"topics/21-linux-production-debug.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":22,"file":"topics/22-low-level-design-ood.json","topic_type":"algorithm"},{"n":23,"file":"topics/23-java-concurrency-coding.json","topic_type":"algorithm"},{"n":24,"file":"topics/24-domain-driven-design.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":25,"topic_type":"microservice","file":"topics/25-microservice.json"},{"n":26,"file":"topics/26-testing-strategy.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":28,"file":"topics/28-distributed-lock-lease.json","topic_type":"design"}],"row":{"n":22,"file":"topics/22-low-level-design-ood.json","topic_type":"algorithm"},"meta":{"topic_type":"algorithm","key":"22-low-level-design-ood","vi":{"label":"Low-level design / OOD","title":"Low-level design — thiết kế class và viết ra code","intro":"Vòng LLD nằm giữa DSA và system design: không hỏi thuật toán khó, cũng không vẽ kiến trúc trên mây, mà bắt bạn chia class và viết code chạy được trong 45 phút. Rakuten, Grab, Agoda và phần lớn công ty Nhật/global đều có vòng này.","tags":["LRU cache","Rate limiter","SOLID","Strategy / State","Thread-safety","TTL store","Class diagram"]},"en":{"label":"Low-level design / OOD","title":"Low-level design — designing the classes and writing the code","intro":"The LLD round sits between DSA and system design: no hard algorithms, no architecture in the clouds — just split the classes and write working code in 45 minutes. Rakuten, Grab, Agoda and most Japanese and global companies run this round.","tags":["LRU cache","Rate limiter","SOLID","Strategy / State","Thread-safety","TTL store","Class diagram"]},"created_at":"2026-07-31","updated_at":"2026-10-25"},"topicMeta":{"1":{"en":{"label":"Java Core & JVM"},"vi":{"label":"Java Core & JVM"}},"2":{"en":{"label":"Java 8→25 & Java vs Go"},"vi":{"label":"Java 8→25 & Java vs Go"}},"3":{"en":{"label":"Spring Boot deep dive + build"},"vi":{"label":"Spring Boot deep + build"}},"4":{"en":{"label":"REST/gRPC & WebFlux"},"vi":{"label":"REST/gRPC & WebFlux"}},"5":{"en":{"label":"DB core: indexes & locking"},"vi":{"label":"DB core: index & lock"}},"6":{"en":{"label":"DB scaling"},"vi":{"label":"DB scaling"}},"7":{"en":{"label":"SQL/NoSQL & DB engines"},"vi":{"label":"SQL/NoSQL & DB engines"}},"8":{"en":{"label":"Message queue"},"vi":{"label":"Message queue"}},"9":{"en":{"label":"Distributed tx (fintech)"},"vi":{"label":"Distributed tx (fintech)"}},"10":{"en":{"label":"System design + rate limit"},"vi":{"label":"System design + rate limit"}},"11":{"en":{"label":"System design cases"},"vi":{"label":"System design cases"}},"12":{"en":{"label":"Architecture & patterns"},"vi":{"label":"Architecture & patterns"}},"13":{"en":{"label":"Security & OAuth2"},"vi":{"label":"Security & OAuth2"}},"14":{"en":{"label":"DevOps · K8s · best practices"},"vi":{"label":"DevOps · K8s · best practices"}},"15":{"en":{"label":"Network & I/O models"},"vi":{"label":"Network & I/O models"}},"16":{"en":{"label":"Project · concurrency · whiteboard"},"vi":{"label":"Project · concurrency · whiteboard"}},"17":{"en":{"label":"REST API design"},"vi":{"label":"Thiết kế REST API"}},"18":{"en":{"label":"Query optimisation"},"vi":{"label":"Tối ưu query"}},"19":{"en":{"label":"DSA & LeetCode"},"vi":{"label":"DSA & LeetCode"}},"20":{"en":{"label":"Observability & SRE"},"vi":{"label":"Observability & SRE"}},"21":{"en":{"label":"Linux & production debug"},"vi":{"label":"Linux & production debug"}},"22":{"en":{"label":"Low-level design / OOD"},"vi":{"label":"Low-level design / OOD"}},"23":{"en":{"label":"Java concurrency coding"},"vi":{"label":"Java concurrency coding"}},"24":{"en":{"label":"Domain-Driven Design"},"vi":{"label":"Domain-Driven Design"}},"25":{"en":{"label":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track"},"vi":{"label":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track"}},"26":{"en":{"label":"Testing"},"vi":{"label":"Testing"}},"27":{"en":{"label":"API Gateway & Identity Edge"},"vi":{"label":"API Gateway & Identity Edge"}},"28":{"en":{"label":"Distributed Lock & Lease"},"vi":{"label":"Distributed Lock & Lease"}}},"reviewedAt":{"22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q5":"2026-09-12","22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q5":"2026-10-02","22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q7":"2026-10-02","22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q8":"2026-10-02","22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q1":"2026-09-12","22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q2":"2026-09-12","22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q3":"2026-09-12","22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q1":"2026-09-12","22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q2":"2026-09-12","22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q4":"2026-09-12"},"lang":"en","body":{"sections":[{"title":"The LLD framework & classic problems","items":[{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q1","q":"How does a low-level design interview differ from system design, and what five-step framework should you use?","a":"<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>System design (HLD)</th><th>Low-level design (LLD)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Unit</td><td>service, database, queue, cache</td><td>class, interface, method</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Focus</td><td>scale, consistency, partitioning</td><td>responsibilities, coupling, extensibility</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Deliverable</td><td>diagram + trade-offs</td><td><b>working code</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Follow-up</td><td>\"What happens at 10× traffic?\"</td><td>\"How many places change when type X is added?\"</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n**Five-step framework (a communication and invariant workflow; timeboxes are adjustable):**\n\n1. **Clarify the scope** — LLD prompts are deliberately ambiguous: *\"Elevator: how many cars? Priority floors? Scheduling only, or a full simulation?\"* Agree on the smallest scope, actors, and success invariants that fit the interview; three to five minutes is a useful example, not a ritual.\n2. **List use cases** — identify what each actor can do. These become the methods later.\n3. **Identify entities and responsibilities** — nouns become classes and verbs become methods. State explicitly: *\"This class has the single responsibility of…\"*\n4. **Sketch the API before implementing it** — write method signatures and interfaces, [[g:confirm them]], then fill in method bodies. Avoid writing 40 lines before discovering that the prompt was misunderstood.\n5. **Code the core** — implement the most interesting part as working code; describe repetitive parts as *\"analogous\"* and move on.\n\n:::tip What the interviewer actually evaluates\nThe [[o:extensibility of the design]]. A common follow-up is *\"What if we add another vehicle, fee, or strategy type?\"* If the answer is \"add one class implementing the existing interface\", the design is easy to extend. If it is \"edit the `switch` in three places\", the design has a change hotspot.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- [[r:do not draw an ERD]]. LLD asks for the in-memory *object* model, not a schema. Jumping to tables and foreign keys immediately leaves the problem's scope.\n- begin with interfaces and enums, and defer storage; an in-memory `Map` is more than enough unless the prompt says otherwise\n- state the system [[g:invariants]], such as *\"a seat cannot be both FREE and HELD\"*, then show where the code enforces them. This distinguishes a senior from a mid-level answer.\n- prefer [[o:composition over inheritance]]: a deep inheritance tree signals immature design. A four-level `Car extends Vehicle` hierarchy for a \"vehicle types\" problem almost always has a better composition-based alternative.\n- manage time with a heuristic: reserve enough time for a working core, then adjust clarification, design, coding, and extension discussion to the prompt. [[r:Running out of time without working code]] is the worst outcome, so implement the core early.\n- always ask *\"Does this need to be thread-safe?\"* If yes, explain the strategy (22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q3); if no, mention that concurrency was considered.\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q2","q":"How do you design an LRU cache with O(1) get and put, and why do you choose those data structures?","a":"A common LLD exercise (LeetCode 146) asks for [[g:O(1)]] `get` and `put` under expected hash-map performance.\n\nAssumptions matter: this example is process-local, uses non-null keys/values, and requires capacity > 0. A concurrent variant needs an explicit synchronization policy; the complexity claim excludes lock contention and resize spikes.\n\n**Why two structures are required:** a `HashMap` provides O(1) lookup but no order; a list provides order but O(n) lookup. Combine them so the map points [[o:directly to a list node]], making lookup, removal, and insertion all O(1).\n\nIt must be a [[g:doubly]] linked list because removing a node requires its predecessor. Two sentinel nodes (`head`, `tail`) eliminate every special null case.\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"k\">class</span> LRUCache {\n    <span class=\"k\">private final</span> <span class=\"k\">int</span> cap;\n    <span class=\"k\">private final</span> Map&lt;Integer, Node&gt; map = <span class=\"k\">new</span> HashMap&lt;&gt;();\n    <span class=\"k\">private final</span> Node head = <span class=\"k\">new</span> Node(<span class=\"n\">0</span>, <span class=\"n\">0</span>), tail = <span class=\"k\">new</span> Node(<span class=\"n\">0</span>, <span class=\"n\">0</span>);\n<span class=\"c\">    </span>\n    <span class=\"f\">LRUCache</span>(<span class=\"k\">int</span> cap) {\n        <span class=\"k\">this</span>.cap = cap;\n        head.next = tail; tail.prev = head;      <span class=\"c\">// two sentinel nodes</span>\n    }\n<span class=\"c\">    </span>\n    <span class=\"k\">public</span> <span class=\"k\">int</span> <span class=\"f\">get</span>(<span class=\"k\">int</span> key) {\n        Node n = map.<span class=\"f\">get</span>(key);\n        <span class=\"k\">if</span> (n == <span class=\"k\">null</span>) <span class=\"k\">return</span> -<span class=\"n\">1</span>;\n        <span class=\"f\">moveToFront</span>(n);                          <span class=\"c\">// just used =&gt; most recent</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span> n.val;\n    }\n<span class=\"c\">    </span>\n    <span class=\"k\">public</span> <span class=\"k\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">put</span>(<span class=\"k\">int</span> key, <span class=\"k\">int</span> val) {\n        Node n = map.<span class=\"f\">get</span>(key);\n        <span class=\"k\">if</span> (n != <span class=\"k\">null</span>) { n.val = val; <span class=\"f\">moveToFront</span>(n); <span class=\"k\">return</span>; }\n        <span class=\"k\">if</span> (map.<span class=\"f\">size</span>() == cap) {                  <span class=\"c\">// full =&gt; evict the last node</span>\n            Node lru = tail.prev;\n            <span class=\"f\">remove</span>(lru); map.<span class=\"f\">remove</span>(lru.key);\n        }\n        Node fresh = <span class=\"k\">new</span> Node(key, val);\n        map.<span class=\"f\">put</span>(key, fresh); <span class=\"f\">addFirst</span>(fresh);\n    }\n<span class=\"c\">    </span>\n    <span class=\"k\">private</span> <span class=\"k\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">moveToFront</span>(Node n) { <span class=\"f\">remove</span>(n); <span class=\"f\">addFirst</span>(n); }\n    <span class=\"k\">private</span> <span class=\"k\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">remove</span>(Node n) { n.prev.next = n.next; n.next.prev = n.prev; }\n    <span class=\"k\">private</span> <span class=\"k\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">addFirst</span>(Node n) {\n        n.next = head.next; n.prev = head;\n        head.next.prev = n; head.next = n;\n    }\n<span class=\"c\">    </span>\n    <span class=\"k\">private static class</span> Node {\n        <span class=\"k\">int</span> key, val; Node prev, next;\n        <span class=\"f\">Node</span>(<span class=\"k\">int</span> k, <span class=\"k\">int</span> v) { key = k; val = v; }\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n\nEach node must also store its `key`: during eviction you have the node but need the `key` to remove its map entry.\n\n:::deep\n- `LinkedHashMap` can implement this compactly with `accessOrder=true` and an overridden `removeEldestEntry`, but access-order reads mutate the list and the class is not thread-safe.\n- `ConcurrentHashMap` alone does not preserve LRU order; a concurrent design needs an explicit lock or a sharded policy whose contract is understood.\n- for **thread safety**, one global lock is simple but blocks all operations. Sharding can reduce contention, but library internals and eviction guarantees are provider/version-specific; state the contract and measure it instead of assuming every implementation has multiple segments.\n- LRU is [[r:not always the best policy]]: one full-table scan can evict all hot data. [[o:LFU]] or [[o:W-TinyLFU]] can resist scans at the cost of frequency-tracking state; see the admission-versus-eviction design in (25-microservice.05-caching-pitfalls.q10).\n- adding TTL requires a third structure, such as an expiry min-heap or timing wheel; see 22.8\n- in practice, Redis uses [[o:approximate]] LRU by sampling several random keys and evicting the oldest, avoiding a linked list for millions of keys. Mentioning this earns points.\n- useful test cases include capacity = 1, overwriting an existing key, getting a missing key, and verifying eviction order after interleaved get/put operations\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q3","q":"How do you design and code token-bucket and sliding-window rate limiters?","a":"Topic 10 discusses the algorithms at system level; here you must [[o:write the code]].\n\n**Token bucket** — it permits bounded bursts, which is useful when the workload allows bursts; no single limiter algorithm is universally best.\n\nThe examples below assume a process-local bucket, capacity > 0, refill rate >= 0, and `0 &lt; n &lt;= capacity`. Inject a monotonic elapsed-time source; wall-clock time is not appropriate for measuring a duration.\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"k\">class</span> TokenBucket {\n    <span class=\"k\">private final</span> <span class=\"k\">long</span> capacity;\n    <span class=\"k\">private final</span> <span class=\"k\">double</span> refillPerMs;\n    <span class=\"k\">private final</span> LongSupplier nowMs;\n    <span class=\"k\">private</span> <span class=\"k\">double</span> tokens;\n    <span class=\"k\">private</span> <span class=\"k\">long</span> lastRefill;\n<span class=\"c\">    </span>\n    <span class=\"f\">TokenBucket</span>(<span class=\"k\">long</span> capacity, <span class=\"k\">double</span> refillPerSec, LongSupplier nowMs) {\n        <span class=\"k\">this</span>.capacity = capacity;\n        <span class=\"k\">this</span>.nowMs = nowMs;\n        <span class=\"k\">this</span>.refillPerMs = refillPerSec / <span class=\"n\">1000.0</span>;\n        <span class=\"k\">this</span>.tokens = capacity;                   <span class=\"c\">// full at initialization</span>\n        <span class=\"k\">this</span>.lastRefill = nowMs.<span class=\"f\">getAsLong</span>();\n    }\n<span class=\"c\">    </span>\n    <span class=\"k\">synchronized</span> <span class=\"k\">boolean</span> <span class=\"f\">tryAcquire</span>(<span class=\"k\">int</span> n) {\n        <span class=\"f\">refill</span>();\n        <span class=\"k\">if</span> (tokens &lt; n) <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"k\">false</span>;\n        tokens -= n;\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"k\">true</span>;\n    }\n<span class=\"c\">    </span>\n    <span class=\"k\">private</span> <span class=\"k\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">refill</span>() {\n        <span class=\"k\">long</span> now = nowMs.<span class=\"f\">getAsLong</span>();\n        tokens = Math.<span class=\"f\">min</span>(capacity, tokens + (now - lastRefill) * refillPerMs);\n        lastRefill = now;                          <span class=\"c\">// clamp at capacity: idle time cannot accumulate without bound</span>\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n\nUse [[o:lazy refill]], calculated when called, rather than a periodic refill thread. It requires no dedicated refill thread and remains accurate to the millisecond.\n\n**Sliding window log** — exact, at the cost of O(requests in the window) memory:\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"k\">boolean</span> <span class=\"f\">allow</span>(String key, <span class=\"k\">int</span> limit, <span class=\"k\">long</span> windowMs, LongSupplier nowMs) {\n    <span class=\"k\">long</span> now = nowMs.<span class=\"f\">getAsLong</span>();\n    Deque&lt;Long&gt; q = log.<span class=\"f\">computeIfAbsent</span>(key, k -&gt; <span class=\"k\">new</span> ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;());\n    <span class=\"k\">synchronized</span> (q) {\n        <span class=\"k\">while</span> (!q.<span class=\"f\">isEmpty</span>() &amp;&amp; q.<span class=\"f\">peekFirst</span>() &lt;= now - windowMs) q.<span class=\"f\">pollFirst</span>();\n        <span class=\"k\">if</span> (q.<span class=\"f\">size</span>() &gt;= limit) <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"k\">false</span>;\n        q.<span class=\"f\">addLast</span>(now);\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"k\">true</span>;\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>Memory</th><th>Allows bursts</th><th>Accuracy</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Token bucket</td><td>O(1)</td><td>yes, up to capacity</td><td>high</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fixed window</td><td>O(1)</td><td>yes, up to 2× at the boundary</td><td><b>low</b> — boundary error</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sliding window log</td><td>O(n)</td><td>no</td><td>exact</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sliding window counter</td><td>O(1)</td><td>limited</td><td>good approximation</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n:::deep\n- [[r:fixed-window boundary error]]: with a limit of 100 per minute, 100 requests at 10:00:59 and another 100 at 10:01:00 produce 200 requests in one second while remaining \"within the rules\". This is a useful boundary test.\n- in a distributed system, state must live in [[g:Redis]], and the read-modify-write operation must be atomic through a Lua script or `INCR` plus `EXPIRE`. Per-instance in-memory limits become N times the intended limit across N instances.\n- a Redis token bucket stores `tokens` and `lastRefill` in a hash and wraps refill plus deduction in one Lua script\n- return the correct [[o:429]] status with `Retry-After` and `X-RateLimit-Limit/Remaining/Reset` headers (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q2)\n- choose the key by layer: API key for partners, user for authenticated consumers, and IP for anonymous users. [[r:IP-only limiting is wrong]] when customers behind NAT or CGNAT share an address.\n- distinguish rate limiting, which prevents abuse, from [[o:load shedding]], which protects against overload using current load, and from concurrency limits. Real systems commonly need all three.\n- the hard testing problem is freezing time. Inject `Clock` or `Supplier&lt;Long&gt;` instead of calling `System.currentTimeMillis()` directly; this makes the code testable and demonstrates good design.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q4","q":"How should classes be divided when designing a parking lot or elevator system?","a":"These are two classic problems. Evaluation is not about one \"correct answer\" but about [[o:responsibility boundaries]] and the ability to add new types.\n\n**Parking lot — a compact model:**\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"k\">enum</span> SpotType { MOTORBIKE, COMPACT, LARGE }\n<span class=\"c\">//</span>\n<span class=\"k\">interface</span> Vehicle { SpotType <span class=\"f\">requiredSpot</span>(); String <span class=\"f\">plate</span>(); }\n<span class=\"c\">//</span>\n<span class=\"k\">interface</span> PricingStrategy { Money <span class=\"f\">price</span>(Ticket t, Instant out); }\n<span class=\"k\">interface</span> SpotAllocator { Optional&lt;Spot&gt; <span class=\"f\">allocate</span>(Vehicle v); }\n<span class=\"c\">//</span>\n<span class=\"k\">class</span> ParkingLot {\n    <span class=\"k\">private final</span> SpotAllocator allocator;      <span class=\"c\">// change the allocation policy</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">private final</span> PricingStrategy pricing;      <span class=\"c\">// change the pricing policy</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">public</span> Ticket <span class=\"f\">park</span>(Vehicle v) { ... }\n    <span class=\"k\">public</span> Money <span class=\"f\">unpark</span>(String ticketId) { ... }\n}</code></pre>\n\nThose two `interface` declarations answer the extension question *\"What if pricing changes during peak hours?\"*: add another `PricingStrategy` and [[g:do not modify `ParkingLot`]].\n\n**Elevator — the key distinction is separating *commands* from *scheduling*:**\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"k\">enum</span> Direction { UP, DOWN, IDLE }\n<span class=\"c\">//</span>\n<span class=\"k\">class</span> Request { <span class=\"k\">int</span> floor; Direction dir; }        <span class=\"c\">// hall call</span>\n<span class=\"k\">class</span> Elevator {                                <span class=\"c\">// only knows how to move ITSELF</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">int</span> current; Direction dir;\n    TreeSet&lt;Integer&gt; stops;\n    <span class=\"k\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">step</span>() { ... }                          <span class=\"c\">// move one floor</span>\n}\n<span class=\"k\">interface</span> Scheduler {                           <span class=\"c\">// choose which car serves the request</span>\n    Elevator <span class=\"f\">assign</span>(Request r, List&lt;Elevator&gt; cabins);\n}</code></pre>\n\nAn `Elevator` must not know about other cars. If it does, it violates single responsibility and cannot be tested independently.\n\n:::deep\n- the [[r:number-one mistake]] is putting everything in a 300-line `ParkingLot` class with `if (type == ...)` scattered throughout. An extension question exposes it immediately.\n- [[o:state the invariants aloud]]: one vehicle occupies one spot, one spot holds one vehicle, and a paid ticket cannot be paid again. Then show where the code enforces them.\n- allocate efficiently with `Map&lt;SpotType, PriorityQueue&lt;Spot&gt;&gt;` in O(log n) instead of scanning every spot. Complexity analysis earns points even in an LLD round.\n- for elevators, use the [[g:SCAN/elevator]] algorithm, traveling fully in one direction before reversing. Disk I/O scheduling uses the same idea, and connecting it to operating systems is valuable.\n- a `TreeSet` for `stops` turns \"the next stop in the current direction\" into O(log n) `ceiling()`/`floor()` calls, cleaner than manually sorting\n- concurrency matters when many people press buttons simultaneously (22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q3). At minimum say, *\"I will use a request queue and one dispatcher thread.\"*\n- the same template solves vending machines (State pattern), cinema reservations (seat locking), chat, and libraries. Mastering the template matters more than memorizing each problem.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.the-lld-framework-classic-problems.q5","difficulty":"advanced","q":"Take-home brief: a Q&A REST service where users ask questions, answer open ones, and up- or down-vote both. How do you keep the score right under concurrent votes, and how do you scope three days?","a":"**Read the brief as a contract.** Create questions, list open ones, answer, vote ±1 on questions and answers; tags and sign-up are optional. CRUD is not the hard part — a vote is *one per user per post*, and the score must agree with the votes.\n\n**API:**\n<pre><code>POST   /questions                    {title, body, tags[]}    → 201 + Location\nGET    /questions?status=open&amp;tag=java&amp;cursor=…                → one page\nPOST   /questions/{id}/answers       {body}                   → 201\nPUT    /posts/{id}/vote              {\"value\": 1 | -1}        → 200 {score, myVote}\nDELETE /posts/{id}/vote                                       → 200 {score, myVote: 0}</code></pre>\n\n`PUT` because a vote is *the state of my vote*, not an event: sending it twice is harmless, so a retry is safe without an idempotency key (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q1). Questions and answers share a `post` table with a `kind`, so a vote has one foreign key.\n\n**The constraint is the rule, and a changed vote moves the score by the difference** (+1 to −1 is −2):\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"k\">CREATE TABLE</span> vote (\n  post_id <span class=\"k\">bigint</span>   <span class=\"k\">NOT NULL REFERENCES</span> post,\n  user_id <span class=\"k\">bigint</span>   <span class=\"k\">NOT NULL</span>,\n  value   <span class=\"k\">smallint NOT NULL CHECK</span> (value <span class=\"k\">IN</span> (-<span class=\"n\">1</span>, <span class=\"n\">1</span>)),\n  <span class=\"k\">PRIMARY KEY</span> (post_id, user_id)            <span class=\"c\">-- one vote per user per post</span>\n);\n<span class=\"c\">──── one transaction; PostgreSQL 18 returns the old value from the upsert</span>\n<span class=\"k\">INSERT INTO</span> vote <span class=\"k\">VALUES</span> ($1, $2, $3)\n<span class=\"k\">ON CONFLICT</span> (post_id, user_id) <span class=\"k\">DO UPDATE SET</span> value = <span class=\"k\">EXCLUDED</span>.value\n<span class=\"k\">RETURNING</span> <span class=\"k\">COALESCE</span>(old.value, <span class=\"n\">0</span>) <span class=\"k\">AS</span> before;    <span class=\"c\">-- old.* is NULL on a fresh insert</span>\n<span class=\"k\">UPDATE</span> post <span class=\"k\">SET</span> score = score + ($3 - :before) <span class=\"k\">WHERE</span> id = $1;   <span class=\"c\">-- relative: no lost update</span></code></pre>\n\nOn an older PostgreSQL or on MySQL, start the transaction with `SELECT … FROM post WHERE id = ? FOR UPDATE`: votes on one post then serialize, and reading the old vote is safe.\n\n**Scope three days by risk, not by feature count:**\n1. day one: the vote path end to end, with a test that fires 50 concurrent votes and asserts `score = SUM(value)`.\n2. day two: questions, answers and the open list with cursor pagination.\n3. day three: `docker compose up` that starts the app and the database with migrations, plus a README listing every assumption. Tags and auth come last.\n\n:::tip What reviewers read first\nThe vote invariant and its test, the README's assumptions, and whether `docker compose up` works on a clean machine. Feature count comes after all three.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- **stored score or computed?** `SUM(value)` over an index on `vote(post_id)` is exact and needs no update path; a stored `score` makes \"sort by score\" an index scan. Store it only when listing by score is required, and keep a reconciliation query that recomputes it.\n- the relative `score = score + delta` is the point: read-modify-write in Java (`post.setScore(post.getScore() + 1)`) loses votes under concurrency unless the row is locked or versioned (05-db-core-index-lock.transactions-mvcc-locking.q3).\n- per tier: at T1–T3 serializing the votes of one post on its row costs nothing anyone notices; at T4 a viral post is a hot row that `needs its own scaling story` — votes appended as events, the score folded asynchronously and shown as approximate (25-microservice.06-hot-partition-db-bottleneck.q1).\n- rules to state as assumptions: no voting on your own post (enforce in the service and answer with a problem-details error), whether closed questions accept votes, and whether a deleted post keeps its votes.\n- \"open\" needs a definition — no accepted answer, or not closed by a moderator. Write it down, index `(status, created_at)` for the list, and page with a cursor (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q5).\n- RFC 9457 error bodies, validation at the controller boundary and Flyway owning the schema make a take-home read like production code (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q2).\n- the same shape — a uniqueness constraint as the rule, a relative counter as the projection — answers likes, follows and reactions in a system-design round.\n:::"}]},{"title":"Patterns in interview code","items":[{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q1","q":"How do you reference SOLID naturally in an LLD interview without sounding formulaic?","a":"Reciting the five letters from memory is [[r:counterproductive]]. Use them correctly by reaching a design decision, [[g:naming the relevant principle, and immediately moving on]].\n\n- [[b:S]] — Single Responsibility. *\"I separated `PricingStrategy` because its reason to change differs from `ParkingLot`.\"* The rule is \"one reason to change\", not \"one method\".\n- [[b:O]] — Open/Closed. *\"Adding a new vehicle type means adding an implementation, not editing existing classes.\"* This is often useful to demonstrate in an extension question.\n- [[b:L]] — Liskov. A subtype must replace its base type without violating expectations. The classic `Square extends Rectangle` fails because `setWidth` also changes the height.\n- [[b:I]] — Interface Segregation. Interfaces should be small and consumer-specific. A 20-method `Repository` forces every implementation to provide all 20 methods.\n- [[b:D]] — Dependency Inversion. `ParkingLot` depends on the `PricingStrategy` abstraction, not the concrete `WeekendPricing`.\n\n:::tip A strong interview line\n*\"I apply Open/Closed [[o:where I expect change]]—the pricing calculation. I keep unlikely-to-change areas simple because premature abstraction is also debt.\"* Explaining a principle's **limits** is what sounds senior.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- the [[r:over-engineering trap]] is creating an interface for every class \"to follow SOLID\". An interface with one implementation that will never gain a second is [[o:noise]], not good design. YAGNI wins until a real axis of change appears.\n- Dependency Inversion is not Dependency Injection. DI is a common *mechanism* for achieving DIP; confusing the concepts is a frequent interview mistake.\n- SRP correctly means *\"group things that change for the same reason and separate things that change for different reasons\"*. It is based on **who or what requests the change**, not the number of tasks a class performs.\n- a common real-world Liskov violation is a subclass throwing `UnsupportedOperationException`, as with `Collections.unmodifiableList`. Recognizing that even standard libraries violate the rule shows understanding rather than memorisation.\n- SOLID arose around inheritance-heavy OOP; modern design leans toward [[o:composition, pure functions, and immutable data]]. Mentioning that perspective demonstrates maturity.\n- combine other principles naturally: DRY, while avoiding merging coincidentally similar code, KISS, YAGNI, and the Law of Demeter\n:::","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q2","q":"When should you choose Strategy, State, or Chain of Responsibility?","a":"The three patterns look similar because all of them plug in behavior, but they answer three different questions.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Change axis</th><th>Prefer</th><th>Signal</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Algorithm/rule changes</td><td>Strategy</td><td>same lifecycle, different way to do the work</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lifecycle/state changes</td><td>State</td><td>legal behavior follows the current state</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Request routing</td><td>Chain</td><td>handlers may accept or forward</td></tr>\n<tr><td>No real change axis</td><td>plain class/function</td><td>an extra pattern would be over-engineering</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Answers</th><th>Who chooses the next behavior</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Strategy</td><td><b>how should it be done?</b> — interchangeable algorithms</td><td>the client chooses; it does not change mid-operation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>State</td><td><b>where are we now?</b> — behavior changes with state</td><td>the current state transitions to the next state</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chain</td><td><b>who handles it?</b> — try handlers in sequence until one accepts</td><td>each handler decides whether to handle or forward</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n**Strategy** — pricing, compression choice, or scheduling algorithm:\n\n<pre><code>lot = <span class=\"k\">new</span> ParkingLot(<span class=\"k\">new</span> WeekendPricing());   <span class=\"c\">// injected at construction</span></code></pre>\n\n**State** — booking lifecycle, vending machine, or order:\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"k\">interface</span> BookingState {\n    BookingState <span class=\"f\">pay</span>(Booking b);       <span class=\"c\">// return the next state</span>\n    BookingState <span class=\"f\">cancel</span>(Booking b);\n}\n<span class=\"c\">//</span>\n<span class=\"k\">class</span> Held <span class=\"k\">implements</span> BookingState {\n    <span class=\"k\">public</span> BookingState <span class=\"f\">pay</span>(Booking b) { b.<span class=\"f\">charge</span>(); <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"k\">new</span> Paid(); }\n    <span class=\"k\">public</span> BookingState <span class=\"f\">cancel</span>(Booking b) { b.<span class=\"f\">releaseSeat</span>(); <span class=\"k\">return</span> <span class=\"k\">new</span> Cancelled(); }\n}</code></pre>\n\nThe benefit is that [[g:invalid transitions become impossible]]: `Cancelled.pay()` can simply throw an error, with no scattered `if` statements.\n\n**Chain of Responsibility** — middleware, filters, or validation pipelines:\n\n<pre><code>auth.<span class=\"f\">setNext</span>(rateLimit).<span class=\"f\">setNext</span>(validate).<span class=\"f\">setNext</span>(handler);</code></pre>\n\n:::deep\n- the signal for State is [[r:the same `if (status == ...)` chain repeated across several methods]]. That is a state machine hidden inside `if` statements.\n- State [[o:holds context]] and transitions itself; Strategy is [[o:stateless]] and selected externally. It is a useful distinction when the lifecycle, not just the algorithm, changes.\n- in modern Java, a Strategy is often just a [[g:lambda]] (`Function`/`Predicate`) rather than a class hierarchy. Saying so shows understanding rather than pattern worship.\n- an `enum` can implement State compactly in Java by overriding methods per constant, especially when states hold no private data\n- a long Chain is [[r:difficult to debug]]; add logs or traces at every link and define whether an unhandled input is an error or is silently ignored\n- patterns commonly paired in LLD include [[o:Factory]] (construct the correct type from input), [[o:Observer]] (notify changes), [[o:Builder]] (objects with many optional arguments), and [[o:Command]] (undo/redo or queued tasks)\n- [[r:do not force patterns]] into a design to show them off. A misplaced pattern is worse than none; the interviewer will ask *\"Why is it needed here?\"*, and the answer must be real.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q3","q":"What thread-safety questions arise in an LLD interview, and how should you answer them?","a":"Almost every LLD problem ends with *\"What if multiple threads call it concurrently?\"* A prepared framework makes the answer quick.\n\n**Four levels, in preferred order:**\n\n1. [[g:Immutability]] — no shared mutable state means no concurrency problem. Mention this first.\n2. [[g:Single-thread confinement]] — funnel work through a queue to one coordinator thread, as in an elevator or actor model.\n3. [[g:Concurrent data structures]] — `ConcurrentHashMap`, `AtomicLong`, and `BlockingQueue`. These are sufficient for most problems.\n4. [[g:Explicit locking]] — `synchronized` or `ReentrantLock`. This is the most powerful and the easiest to get wrong.\n\n**The common interviewer trap** is a compound operation on `ConcurrentHashMap`:\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\">// WRONG: each call is atomic, but the PAIR is not</span>\n<span class=\"k\">if</span> (!map.<span class=\"f\">containsKey</span>(k)) map.<span class=\"f\">put</span>(k, v);\n<span class=\"c\">//</span>\n<span class=\"c\">// CORRECT: one atomic operation</span>\nmap.<span class=\"f\">putIfAbsent</span>(k, v);\nmap.<span class=\"f\">computeIfAbsent</span>(k, key -&gt; <span class=\"k\">new</span> Spot(key));</code></pre>\n\n*\"Using `ConcurrentHashMap` makes it safe\"* is [[r:incorrect]]. It guarantees individual operations, not sequences of operations.\n\n**A correct parking-space allocation example:**\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"k\">public</span> Optional&lt;Spot&gt; <span class=\"f\">allocate</span>(Vehicle v) {\n    Queue&lt;Spot&gt; free = freeByType.<span class=\"f\">get</span>(v.<span class=\"f\">requiredSpot</span>());\n    Spot s = free.<span class=\"f\">poll</span>();                  <span class=\"c\">// ConcurrentLinkedQueue: atomic poll</span>\n    <span class=\"k\">return</span> Optional.<span class=\"f\">ofNullable</span>(s);         <span class=\"c\">// one vehicle = one spot, no lock required</span>\n}</code></pre>\n\n:::deep\n- [[o:minimize lock scope]]: lock an individual spot or key rather than the entire lot. Using `synchronized` on every method of a singleton serializes the entire system.\n- consistent lock ordering is the cheapest deadlock prevention: when two locks are needed, always acquire them by increasing ID (see 5.10 and 23.3)\n- `ReentrantLock` versus `synchronized`: the former provides `tryLock(timeout)`, interruptible acquisition, and multiple `Condition`s (23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q1). Without those requirements, `synchronized` is simpler and well optimized by the JIT.\n- [[r:do not]] make outbound HTTP or database calls while holding a lock; doing so lengthens the critical section and increases deadlock risk\n- for read-heavy, write-light workloads, use `ReadWriteLock`, or copy-on-write when writes are extremely rare (23-java-concurrency-coding.classic-synchronisation-problems.q4)\n- a useful knowledge check: [[o:`volatile`]] guarantees *visibility*, not *atomicity*. `volatile int i; i++` is still broken. This is a classic follow-up question.\n- if the prompt does not require concurrency, say *\"I will design the single-threaded version first for clarity, then identify the synchronisation points.\"* Raising it proactively is better than waiting to be challenged.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q4","q":"How do you design an in-memory key-value store with TTL?","a":"This natural extension of LRU is a miniature Redis and a common extension exercise.\n\n**Requirements:** `put(k, v, ttl)`, `get(k)`, automatic expiry, and no memory leaks.\n\nValidity and cleanup are different contracts: `get` must treat an expired entry as a miss, while cleanup only reclaims space. Lazy expiry therefore needs a bounded sweep, sampling job, or expiry index; a delayed cleanup must not make stale data valid.\n\n**Three expiry approaches — production systems use all three:**\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Trade-off</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Lazy</td><td>check expiry during <code>get</code></td><td>cheap, but an unread key can <b>remain forever</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Active sampling</td><td>a periodic job samples random keys and deletes expired ones</td><td>steady low CPU cost; cleans cold keys</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Expiry min-heap</td><td>a job reads exactly the next key due to expire</td><td>precise, but the heap needs cleanup when keys are overwritten</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"k\">class</span> TtlStore {\n    <span class=\"k\">private final</span> Map&lt;String, Entry&gt; map = <span class=\"k\">new</span> ConcurrentHashMap&lt;&gt;();\n    <span class=\"k\">private final</span> LongSupplier nowMs;\n    <span class=\"f\">TtlStore</span>(LongSupplier nowMs) { <span class=\"k\">this</span>.nowMs = nowMs; }\n    <span class=\"k\">private record</span> Entry(String val, <span class=\"k\">long</span> expireAt) {\n        <span class=\"k\">boolean</span> <span class=\"f\">alive</span>(<span class=\"k\">long</span> now) { <span class=\"k\">return</span> expireAt == <span class=\"n\">0</span> || now &lt; expireAt; }\n    }\n<span class=\"c\">    </span>\n    <span class=\"k\">public</span> <span class=\"k\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">put</span>(String k, String v, <span class=\"k\">long</span> ttlMs) {\n        <span class=\"k\">long</span> exp = ttlMs &lt;= <span class=\"n\">0</span> ? <span class=\"n\">0</span> : nowMs.<span class=\"f\">getAsLong</span>() + ttlMs;\n        map.<span class=\"f\">put</span>(k, <span class=\"k\">new</span> Entry(v, exp));\n    }\n<span class=\"c\">    </span>\n    <span class=\"k\">public</span> Optional&lt;String&gt; <span class=\"f\">get</span>(String k) {\n        <span class=\"k\">long</span> now = nowMs.<span class=\"f\">getAsLong</span>();\n        Entry e = map.<span class=\"f\">get</span>(k);\n        <span class=\"k\">if</span> (e == <span class=\"k\">null</span>) <span class=\"k\">return</span> Optional.<span class=\"f\">empty</span>();\n        <span class=\"k\">if</span> (!e.<span class=\"f\">alive</span>(now)) {\n            map.<span class=\"f\">remove</span>(k, e);                 <span class=\"c\">// remove(k,v): do not delete a newer entry</span>\n            <span class=\"k\">return</span> Optional.<span class=\"f\">empty</span>();\n        }\n        <span class=\"k\">return</span> Optional.<span class=\"f\">of</span>(e.<span class=\"f\">val</span>());\n    }\n}</code></pre>\n\nThe two-argument `map.remove(k, e)` is [[o:important]]: another thread may `put` a new value between the read and delete. Removing by key alone would [[r:delete newly written data]].\n\n:::deep\n- Redis uses exactly lazy expiry plus active sampling: every 100 ms it samples 20 keys with TTLs, deletes expired keys, and repeats if more than 25% were expired. [[g:Mentioning this is a major plus.]]\n- a [[o:timing wheel]], such as Netty's `HashedWheelTimer` or Kafka's implementation, outperforms a min-heap for millions of expirations with O(1) insertion/removal instead of O(log n)\n- combining TTL with LRU requires both a capacity limit and expiry. Caffeine already provides `expireAfterWrite` plus `maximumSize`; mention it to demonstrate library awareness.\n- The injected `nowMs` source must be monotonic for elapsed TTL checks; a wall clock can jump when NTP adjusts it. Persisted/distributed deadlines need a separate absolute-time and clock-skew policy.\n- persistence requires a WAL or snapshots, such as Redis AOF and RDB. It is usually outside a 45-minute scope but worth identifying.\n- a common extension is `getOrLoad` using cache-aside plus [[o:cache-stampede]] protection through `computeIfAbsent` or single-flight, directly connecting to the Microservices caching chapter\n:::","difficulty":"extra"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q5","difficulty":"advanced","q":"How do you design an object model for testability, invariants, and deterministic concurrency tests?","a":"Testability is an architectural property. Keep domain decisions in small objects or pure functions; put clocks, randomness, ID generation, persistence, messaging, and scheduling behind narrow injected ports.\n\nThis lets tests control inputs without exposing private fields or adding production-only switches.\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"k\">interface</span> Clock { Instant <span class=\"f\">now</span>(); }\n<span class=\"k\">interface</span> IdSource { String <span class=\"f\">next</span>(); }\n<span class=\"k\">final class</span> HoldService {\n    <span class=\"k\">private final</span> Clock clock;\n    <span class=\"k\">private final</span> HoldRepository holds;\n    <span class=\"c\">// constructor injection; no Instant.now() hidden in domain logic</span>\n}</code></pre>\n\nExpress each aggregate invariant and legal transition close to the state it protects: a hold cannot expire before creation, a released hold cannot be confirmed, balance-changing commands must preserve the ledger equation.\n\nReturn explicit outcomes instead of using exceptions for every expected rejection. Make side effects occur after a decision through ports, and define idempotency where commands can be retried.\n\nUse a layered test portfolio:\n\n- [[b:Example/unit tests]] cover boundaries and state transitions with a fake clock and deterministic IDs.\n- [[b:Property/model-based tests]] generate command sequences and assert invariants after every step; a simple reference model can catch optimized-implementation mistakes.\n- [[b:Contract/integration tests]] verify adapters against the real database, broker, serializer, or HTTP contract.\n- [[b:Concurrency tests]] first identify the linearization point, then coordinate targeted interleavings with barriers/latches and bounded timeouts. Add sustained stress or OpenJDK jcstress as exploratory stress evidence for Java Memory Model questions; a passing run is evidence, not proof.\n\n:::deep\nDo not use `Thread.sleep` as synchronization. Inject virtual time for TTL and refill logic; use explicit signals to know when a worker reached the intended point. Record seeds and shrink failing property cases. Assert final state and externally visible history, not implementation timing.\n\nThread safety is part of the public contract: document which methods are safe concurrently, compound-operation semantics, callback/reentrancy rules, fairness, blocking, cancellation, and ownership of returned mutable data.\n\nPrefer designs with a single owner or immutable snapshots when they make illegal interleavings unrepresentable. The concurrency reasoning framework is covered in (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q3).\n:::"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q6","q":"What must a correct in-memory TTL cache specify beyond O(1) get and put?","a":"O(1) lookup is only the data-structure part. A correct TTL cache needs an explicit contract for time, visibility, cleanup, replacement, capacity, restart and concurrency.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Safe default to discuss</th><th>Failure if omitted</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Expiration time</td><td>Store an absolute deadline; use an injected monotonic elapsed-time source for in-process expiry</td><td>Wall-clock jumps make entries live too long or expire early</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Read semantics</td><td>`get` checks the deadline and treats expired data as a miss</td><td>Expired data is returned until a background thread happens to clean it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cleanup</td><td>Lazy cleanup plus bounded scheduled/eviction work; cleanup is not validity</td><td>Expired keys leak memory or a cleanup scan creates latency spikes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Replacement race</td><td>Version/generation or compare-and-remove when deleting an expired entry</td><td>Old cleanup deletes a newer value written for the same key</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Restart/distribution</td><td>State whether data is disposable and process-local; use a shared authority only when the contract needs it</td><td>A local cache is mistaken for a lock, source of truth, or cross-instance rate limit</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n:::deep\n- `java.time.Clock` is injectable but represents wall-clock time. For elapsed TTL in one process, inject a monotonic ticker backed by `System.nanoTime`; for persisted/distributed deadlines, define clock-skew and absolute-time behavior explicitly.\n- ConcurrentHashMap makes individual map operations safe, not the whole `get-or-refresh`, `put-if-newer`, or stale-cleanup workflow. Use `compute`, a lock/actor, or a versioned compare-and-remove around the invariant.\n- A timer is an optimization. Correctness must remain true if cleanup is delayed, duplicated, reordered, or stopped.\n- In Redis or another shared cache, TTL/eviction/replication are provider contracts; they do not automatically provide fencing, durable truth or business idempotency. Link distributed leases to Topic 28 and API semantics to Topic 17.\n- Tests should advance a fake ticker, race expiry against replacement, restart the process, bound memory, and verify metrics for hit/miss/expired/evicted/stale-cleanup outcomes.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q7","difficulty":"core","q":"How do you write a thread-safe singleton in Java, why is double-checked locking broken without `volatile`, and when should you not write one at all?","a":"**Three correct versions**, starting with the one to reach for first:\n\n<pre><code><span class=\"c\">// 1. enum — one line, safe against serialization and reflection</span>\n<span class=\"k\">enum</span> Registry { INSTANCE; <span class=\"k\">void</span> <span class=\"f\">register</span>(String key) { … } }\n<span class=\"c\">──── 2. holder class — lazy and lock-free: the JVM runs Holder's static init once, on first use</span>\n<span class=\"k\">final class</span> Pricing {\n    <span class=\"k\">private</span> Pricing() {}\n    <span class=\"k\">private static class</span> Holder { <span class=\"k\">static final</span> Pricing I = <span class=\"k\">new</span> Pricing(); }\n    <span class=\"k\">static</span> Pricing <span class=\"f\">get</span>() { <span class=\"k\">return</span> Holder.I; }\n}\n<span class=\"c\">──── 3. double-checked locking — for a lazy INSTANCE field that cannot be static</span>\n<span class=\"k\">private volatile</span> Config cfg;                     <span class=\"r\">// volatile is the whole fix</span>\nConfig <span class=\"f\">config</span>() {\n    Config c = cfg;\n    <span class=\"k\">if</span> (c == <span class=\"k\">null</span>) {\n        <span class=\"k\">synchronized</span> (<span class=\"k\">this</span>) {\n            c = cfg;\n            <span class=\"k\">if</span> (c == <span class=\"k\">null</span>) cfg = c = <span class=\"f\">load</span>();\n        }\n    }\n    <span class=\"k\">return</span> c;\n}</code></pre>\n\n**Why DCL without `volatile` breaks:** `cfg = new Config()` is not one step. With no happens-before edge, another thread can see the reference before the constructor's writes, skip the lock because the field is non-null, and read a half-built object (01-java-core-jvm.memory-execution-model.q1).\n\n`volatile` supplies that edge: writing the reference happens-before every later read of it, and the constructor's writes come with it. That guarantee dates from the Java 5 memory model; before it, DCL was broken even with `volatile`.\n\n:::tip In a Spring service, don't write one\nA singleton-scoped bean is one instance per container, created thread-safely and replaceable in a test. A static GoF singleton is global state a test cannot swap out.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- eager `private static final X I = new X();` is correct and usually enough — laziness only matters when construction is expensive and the class loads long before first use.\n- `synchronized` on the whole getter is correct too. The objection is not speed (an uncontended lock is cheap) but that the holder idiom makes it unnecessary.\n- the enum form alone resists serialization (otherwise `readResolve` is needed) and reflective constructor calls (Effective Java, Item 3). Its limit: it cannot extend a class.\n- a singleton is per class loader, not per JVM: two web apps, or a hot-reload loader, give you two (01-java-core-jvm.language-fundamentals.q8).\n- the local variable `c` in DCL is not decoration: the fast path reads the volatile field once instead of twice.\n- the strongest reason to avoid the pattern is testability — hidden global state ties every caller to one implementation (22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q5).\n:::"},{"id":"22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q8","difficulty":"core","q":"What should a team's coding conventions actually enforce — and which rules belong in a tool rather than a review comment?","a":"Conventions exist so a reviewer spends attention on design, not on spacing. That splits them in two.\n\n**Mechanical — a tool fails the build and nobody argues in review:**\n- formatting: one formatter (google-java-format through Spotless, or a checked-in IDE profile), run on commit and in CI.\n- style a parser can see: Checkstyle with a shared config, Google's or Sun's as the base.\n- bug patterns: Error Prone; nullness through JSpecify annotations checked by NullAway — the annotations Spring Framework 7 and Boot 4 adopted.\n\n**Judgement — written down once, applied in review:**\n\n<table>\n<tr><th>Rule</th><th>Good</th><th>Why</th></tr>\n<tr><td>names say purpose, not form</td><td><code>FILE_PATH_SEPARATOR = \"/\"</code><br>not <code>SLASH</code></td><td>the value may change; the meaning should not</td></tr>\n<tr><td>booleans read as a question</td><td><code>isActive</code> · <code>hasOrders()</code> · <code>canCheckout()</code></td><td><code>if (order.canCheckout())</code> reads as a sentence</td></tr>\n<tr><td>one verb per act</td><td>always <code>findBy…</code>, never also <code>get…</code> or <code>select…</code> for the same lookup</td><td>a synonym makes the reader hunt for a difference</td></tr>\n<tr><td>domain words in code</td><td>the term the business uses</td><td>the ubiquitous language (24-domain-driven-design.strategic-splitting-the-domain.q1)</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n**Null is a design decision, not a check sprinkled everywhere.** Decide which values may be absent, mark them, and validate at the boundary. `Optional` belongs on return types — the JDK's own note calls it \"primarily intended for use as a method return type\" — not on fields, parameters or collections.\n\n<pre><code>Optional&lt;Customer&gt; <span class=\"f\">findByEmail</span>(String email);       <span class=\"c\">// absence is a real answer</span>\nList&lt;Order&gt; <span class=\"f\">ordersOf</span>(CustomerId id);                 <span class=\"c\">// empty list, never null</span>\n<span class=\"r\">void notify(Optional&lt;String&gt; channel)</span>              <span class=\"c\">// use an overload or a default</span>\n<span class=\"r\">if (!ObjectUtils.isEmpty(x)) x.run();</span>             <span class=\"c\">// hides why x could be null</span></code></pre>\n\n:::tip The test for a convention\nIf a tool can check it, automate it; if it needs judgement, write the reason beside the rule. A rule with no reason gets broken by the next person who has one.\n:::\n\n:::deep\n- \"never a `for` loop, always streams\" is a dogma to drop: streams win for transform-filter-collect pipelines; a loop is clearer with an early exit, checked exceptions or index arithmetic. Choose per case, and keep side effects out of `map` and `filter`.\n- a [[g:first-class collection]] — a class wrapping one collection with the behaviour on it (`Orders.totalPaid()`) — gives that behaviour one home; worth it once the same filtering appears in three places.\n- getters only where a caller needs them, and no setters on domain objects: behaviour lives on the object, rather than data pulled out and decided elsewhere — the anemic model (12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q3).\n- \"a method does one thing\" is about one level of abstraction, not a line count; a long `if/else` over a type is a strategy, or a `switch` over a sealed type, waiting to happen (12-architecture-patterns.patterns-principles.q7).\n- a constant lives next to its only user (`private static final`); a project-wide `Constants` class becomes a junk drawer that every module depends on.\n- comments explain *why* — a decision, a constraint, a workaround and its ticket. A comment that repeats the code goes stale the first time the code changes.\n- a quality gate in CI (Sonar, Checkstyle) works only if a red gate blocks the merge; a gate people merge past teaches them to ignore it.\n:::"}]}]},"hasVi":true,"stem":"22-low-level-design-ood"